readings.c1s1:
¶
'''12.''' Of a musical instrument, to cause to sound; play: usually applied to instruments that are sounded by striking or twanging, but extended to others.

{{smaller block|''Touch'' thy instrument a strain or two.
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., iv. 3. 257.}}}}

{{smaller block|I'll ''touch'' my horn.
{{right|''Massinger'', Guardian, ii. 4.}}}}

'''13.''' To perform on an instrument, as a piece of music.

{{smaller block|A person in the royal retinue ''touched'' a light and lively air on the flageolet.
{{right|''Scott.''}}}}

'''14.''' To paint or form by touches or strokes as of a pen or brush; mark or delineate by light touches or strokes, as an artist.

{{smaller block|Such heavenly touches ne'er ''touch'd'' earthly faces.
{{right|''Shak.'', Sonnets, xvii.}}}}

{{smaller block|The lines, though ''touch'd'' but faintly, are drawn right.
{{right|''Pope'', Essay on Criticism, l. 22.}}}}

'''15.''' To improve or finish, as a drawing, by adding a stroke here and there, as with a pen, pencil, or brush; retouch: usually with ''up''.

{{smaller block|What he saw was only her natural countenance, ''touched up'' with the usual improvements of an aged Coquette.
{{right|''Addison'', Freeholder, No. 44.}}}}

{{smaller block|My impression [of an engraving] is unequal, being faint in some parts, very dark in others. If the plate was worn, it has been ''touched'' afterwards.
{{right|''N. and Q.'', 7th ser., X. 118.}}}}

'''16.''' To take, as food, drink, etc.; help one's self to; hence, to partake of; taste.

readings.c1s2:
¶
{{smaller block|If thou sytte be a worthyor man
Then thy self, . . .
Suffre hym fyrste to ''towche'' the mete
Ere thy self any ther-of gete.
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 14.}}}}

{{smaller block|He dies that ''touches'' any of this fruit
Till I and my affairs are answered.
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, ii. 7. 98.}}}}

'''17.''' To infect or impair by contact; stain; blot; blemish; taint.

{{smaller block|The life of all his blood
Is ''touch'd'' corruptibly. ''Shak.'', K. John, v. 7. 2.}}

{{smaller block|Thou canst not ''touch'' my credit;
Truth will not suffer me to be abus'd thus.
{{right|''Fletcher'' (''and Massinger''?), Lovers' Progress, iii. 6.}}}}

'''18.''' To impair mentally in some slight degree; affect slightly with craziness: used chiefly in the past participle.

{{smaller block|Madam, you see master's a little—''touched'', that's all. Twenty ounces of blood let loose would set all right again.
{{right|''Vanbrugh'', Confederacy, v. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|Pray mind him not, his brain is ''touch'd''.
{{right|''Steele'', Lying Lover, v. 1.}}}}

'''19†.''' To attack; hence, to animadvert upon; take to task; censure; reprove; ridicule.

{{smaller block|Sur Water Hungerfo and his brother hathe ''touched'' me in iij thinges, but I wolde in no case have ye douches to knowe them for geving hur grefe.
{{right|''Darrell Papers'' (1570) (H. Hall, Society in Elizabethan Age, App., ii.).}}}}

{{smaller block|You teach behaviours!
Or ''touch'' us for our freedoms!
{{right|''Fletcher'', Wildgoose Chase, ii. 3.}}}}

'''20.''' To sting; nettle, as with some sharp speech.

{{smaller block|Beshrew me, but his words have ''touch'd'' me home.
{{right|''Ford'', Perkin Warbeck, ii. 1.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
¶
{{smaller block|The quarrel ''toucheth'' none but us alone.
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., iv. 1. 118.}}}}

{{smaller block|These statutes ''touched'' high and low.
{{right|''J. Gairdner'', Richard III., i.}}}}

26. To swindle; cheat; act dishonestly by: as, to ''touch'' one's mate. [Slang, Australia.]—'''To touch bottom''', {{smaller|to reach the lowest point, especially in price; have the least value.}}—'''To touch elbows.''' {{smaller|See ''elbow''.}}—'''To touch off.''' {{smaller|(''a'') To sketch hastily; finish by a few rapid touches or dashes.}}

{{smaller block|I was upon this whispered, by one of the company who sat next me, that I should now see something ''touched off'' to a nicety.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Clubs.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') To discharge, as a cannon.}}—'''To touch one on the raw.''' {{smaller|See ''raw''<sup>1</sup>.}}—'''To touch the gums''', {{smaller|in ''med.'', to cause incipient salivation by giving mercury.}}—'''To touch the wind''' {{smaller|(''naut.''), to keep the ship as near the wind as possible.}}—'''To touch up.''' {{smaller|(''a'') To repair or improve by slight touches or emendations; retouch: as, to ''touch up'' a picture. (''b'') To remind; jog the memory of. [Colloq.]}}—'''Touched bill of health.''' {{smaller|See ''bill of health'', under ''bill''<sup>3</sup>.}}—'''Touching the ears.''' {{smaller|See ''ear''<sup>1</sup>.}}—'''Touch me not.''' {{smaller|See ''touch-me-not''.}}—'''Touch pot, touch penny''', {{smaller|a proverbial phrase, signifying no credit given.}}

{{smaller block|"We know the custom of such houses," continues he; "'tis ''touch pot, touch penny.''"
{{right|''Graves'', Spiritual Quixote, iii. 2. (''Davies.'')}}}}

II. ''intrans.'' 1. To be in contact; be in a state of junction, so that no appreciable space is between: as, two spheres ''touch'' only in one point.

{{smaller block|Some side by side not ''touching'' walked,<br>As though of happy things they talked.
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, III. 201.}}}}

Specifically—2. To lay the hand or finger upon a person for the purpose of curing a disease, especially scrofula, or king's evil.

{{smaller block|We were then shown Edward the Confessor's tomb;}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|upon which Sir Roger acquainted us that he was the first that ''touched'' for the Evil. {{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 329.}}}}

'''3'''†. To reach; extend.

{{smaller block|The vois of people ''touchede'' to the hevene,
So loude cryden they with mery stevene.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Knight's Tale, l. 1703.}}}}

'''4'''. To make a passing call, as a ship on a voyage: commonly with ''at'', rarely with ''on''.

{{smaller block|And also Pole, which ys xxx myle from Parence, a good havyn, ffor many Shippys and galyes ''towche'' ther rather thanne ''at'' Parence.
{{right|''Torkington'', Diarie of Eng. Travell, p. 16.}}}}

{{smaller block|The next day we ''touched at'' Sidon. {{right|Acts xxvii. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|I made a little voyage round the lake, and ''touched on'' the several towns that lie on its coasts.
{{right|''Addison'', Remarks on Italy (Works, ed. Bohn, I. 510).}}}}

'''5'''. To mention or treat something slightly in discourse; refer cursorily or in passing: commonly with ''on'' or ''upon''.

{{smaller block|Whenne the Sonne is Est in tho partyes, toward Paradys terrestre, it is thanne mydnyght in oure parties o this half, for the rowndenesse of the Erthe, of the whiche I have ''towched'' to zou before. {{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 303.}}}}

{{smaller block|If the antiquaries have ''touched upon'' it, they have immediately quitted it. {{right|''Addison.''}}}}

{{smaller block|The attitude and bearing of the law in this respect, ''on'' which I intend to ''touch'' in quite general terms.
{{right|''Nineteenth Century'', XXVI. 841.}}}}

{{smaller block|As soon as he hath ''touched on'' any science or study, he immediately seems to himself to have mastered it.
{{right|''Bp. Atterbury'', Sermons, I. v.}}}}

{{smaller block|Whenever she ''touch'd on'' me
This brother had laugh'd her down.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Maud, xix. 6.}}}}

'''6'''. To bow or salute by touching the hat or cap. [Prov. Eng.]—'''7'''†. To rob. [Thieves' slang.]—'''8'''†. To stand the test.

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|As in London saith a Iuellere,
Which brought from thence golde oore to us here,
Whereof was fyned mettal good and clene,
As they ''touch,'' no better could be seene.
{{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages,'' I. 199.}}}}

{{smaller block|And now you are brought to the test; ''touch'' right now, soldier,
Now shew the manly pureness of thy mettle.
{{right|''Fletcher,'' Loyal Subject, i. 5.}}}}

9†. To have or take effect; act.

{{smaller block|Strong waters . . . will ''touch'' upon gold that will not ''touch'' upon silver.
{{right|''Bacon.''}}}}

10. ''Naut.,'' of the sails of a square-rigged vessel, to be in such a position that their weather-leeches shake from the ship being steered so close to the wind.—'''To touch and go.''' {{smaller|(''a'') To touch lightly or briefly and pass on; dip in or stop for a moment here and there in course.}}

{{smaller block|As the text doth rise, I will ''touch and go'' a little in every place.
{{right|''Latimer,'' 1st Sermon bef. Edw. VI., 1549.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') ''Naut.,'' to graze the bottom with the keel for a moment, as a vessel under sail, without lessening of the speed.—'''To touch on''' or '''upon.''' See def. 5.—'''Touch and trade papers.''' See ''paper.''}}

'''touch''' (tuch), ''n.'' [< ME. ''touche;'' < ''touch, v.''] 1. That sense by which mechanical pressure upon the surface of the body (the skin, with the lips, the interior of the mouth, etc.) is perceived; sensibility to pressure, weight, and muscular resistance; the sense of feeling; taction. {{smaller|With this is sometimes reckoned sensibility to temperature. The sense of touch is most acute in those parts of the}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|body that are freely movable, especially in the tips of the fingers. It is the most fundamental and least specialized or localized of the senses. See ''tactile corpuscles'', under ''corpuscle''.}}

{{smaller block|Th' ear,
Taste, ''touch'', and smell, pleased from thy table rise.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of A., i. 2. 132.}}}}

{{smaller block|By ''touch'', hard, soft, rough, smooth, we do discern:
By ''touch'', sweet pleasure and sharp pain we try.
{{right|''Sir J. Davies'', Immortal. of Soul, xviii.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Touch'' is . . . the sense by which mechanical force is appreciated, and it presents a strong resemblance to hearing, in which the sensation is excited by intermittent pressures on the auditory organ.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XXIII. 478.}}}}

{{smaller block|All the senses are but modifications of ''touch''.
{{right|''W. Wallace'', Epicureanism, p. 96.}}}}

2. Mental or moral feeling; moral perception or appreciation.

{{smaller block|Can it be
That men should live with such unfeeling souls,
Without or ''touch'' or conscience of religion?
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Case is Altered, v. 3.}}}}

3. Contact.

{{smaller block|Never ''touch'' [was] well welcome to thy hand . . .
Unless I . . . touch'd.
{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., ii. 2. 118.}}}}

{{smaller block|But O, for the ''touch'' of a vanished hand,
And the sound of a voice that is still!
{{right|''Tennyson'', Break, break, break.}}}}

4. Figuratively, a close relation of mutual confidence, sympathy, interest, or the like; sympathy; accord or harmony in relation to common interests: as, to be out of ''touch'' with the times; to keep in ''touch'' with the people.

{{smaller block|The European in Morocco feels that when he is in company with a Barbary Jew he is in ''touch'' with Europe.
{{right|''The Academy'', June 1, 1889, p. 371.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|We want, with our brethren of the working class, that which we have largely lost—the Church I fear not less than those who are outside of it—that expressive thing which we call ''touch''. {{right|''New Princeton Rev.'', II. 47.}}}}

5. Pressure, or application of pressure; impact; a slight stroke, tap, push, or the like: often used figuratively.

{{smaller block|They [the Australians] pray to the Deuill, which hath conference with an Indian vnseene, from a peece of wood; and to him and all the rest many times by night he toucheth the face and breast with cold ''touches'', but they could neuer learne what he was. {{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 864.}}}}

{{smaller block|A little ''touch'' of their adversary gives all that boisterous force the foil. {{right|''B. Jonson'', Alchemist, To the Reader.}}}}

{{smaller block|Vineyards red with the ''touch'' of October. The grapes were gone, but the plants had a color of their own. {{right|''H. James, Jr.'', Little Tour, p. 173.}}}}

6. A slight or brief sound.—7. The impression conveyed to the mind by contact or pressure; effect on the sense of contact with something; feel: as, an object with a slimy ''touch''.—8. A jog; a hint; a reminder; a slight experience.

{{smaller block|The king, your master, knows their disposition very well; a small ''touch'' will put him in mind of them. {{right|''Bacon''.}}}}

{{smaller block|I . . . related unto you y<sup>t</sup> fearfull accidente, or rather judgmente, y<sup>e</sup> Lord pleased to lay on London Bridge, by fire and therin gave you a ''touch'' of my great loss.
{{right|''Sherley'', quoted in Bradford's Plymouth Plantation, p. 308.}}}}

9. A stroke or dash as with a pen, pencil, or brush, literally or figuratively: as, a ''touch'' of bright color; also, any slight added effort or action, such as that expended on some completed work in order to give it finish.

{{smaller block|What strained ''touches'' rhetoric can lend.
{{right|''Shak.'', Sonnets, lxxxii.}}}}

{{smaller block|It tutors nature; artificial strife
Lives in these ''touches'', livelier than life.}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Shak.'', T. of A., i. 1. 38.}}}}

{{smaller block|The old latticed windows, the stone porch, . . . the chimney stacks, were rich in crayon ''touches'', and sepia lights and shades.{{right|''Charlotte Brontë'', Shirley, xi.}}}}

10. Figuratively, something resembling a light stroke or touch. (''a'') A tinge; a smack; a trace: as, a ''touch'' of irony.

{{smaller block|No beast so fierce but knows some ''touch'' of pity.{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., i. 2. 71.}}}}

{{smaller block|An insight into mechanics is desirable, with a ''touch'' of statistics.{{right|''Lamb'', Old and New Schoolmaster.}}}}

{{smaller block|While the air has no ''touch'' of spring,
Bird of promise! we hear thee sing.{{right|''Bryant'', Song Sparrow.}}}}

(''b'') A shade; a trifle; a slight quantity or degree.

{{smaller block|Madam, I have a ''touch'' of your condition,
Which cannot brook the accent of reproof.{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., iv. 4. 157.}}}}

{{smaller block|Bell was a ''touch'' better educated than her husband.{{right|''Mrs. Gaskell'', Sylvia's Lovers, iv.}}}}

(''c'') A taint; a blemish; a defect; an impairment.

{{smaller block|How great a ''touch'' and wound that manner . . . is to his Reputation.{{right|''Sir R. Winwood'', Memorials, I. 448.}}}}

{{smaller block|This ''touch'' in the brain of the British subject is as certainly owing to the reading newspapers as that of the Spanish worthy above-mentioned to the reading works of chivalry.{{right|''Steele'', Tatler, No. 178.}}}}

(''d'') A slight attack or stroke; a twinge; a pang; a feeling: as, a ''touch'' of rheumatism.

{{smaller block|Give me a rose, that I may press its thorns, and prove myself awake by the sharp ''touch'' of pain!{{right|''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, x.}}}}
